const chapters=[
{id:'overview',label:'The world',title:'A world divided. An origin shared.',lede:'Two alliances, one fractured elven ancestry, and a rare bond that threatens to unravel the stories both sides tell.',body:`<div class="grid"><div class="info"><span class="tag">THE SUN ALLIANCE</span><h3>Power in the bloodline</h3><p>Sun Elves and humans share a solar faith and generations of arranged marriages. Their ruling families are bound together by inheritance, diplomacy, and magical ancestry.</p></div><div class="info lunar"><span class="tag">THE UNDERGROUND ALLIANCE</span><h3>Trust built beneath the earth</h3><p>Dark Elves and dwarves share a vast, integrated metropolis. Once wary neighbors, they built a civilization where elven magic and dwarven engineering are inseparable.</p></div></div><h3>The fragile present</h3><p>The alliances live in a tense, often hostile peace. Necessary trade and sensitive diplomacy continue, while old wars, border disputes, and competing histories threaten to reopen the divide.</p><div class="note"><p><strong>The central contradiction</strong><br>Sun Elves consider fated bonds sacred. What happens when that sacred bond chooses someone their society has taught them to distrust?</p></div><h3>What remains of the fey</h3><p>The first elves descended from humans and fey. The fey later vanished, for reasons no civilization understands. Their magic survives in elven blood—and in the rare human lineages that carry their ancestry.</p><p>This compendium follows the latest version of the story bible. Unanswered questions and possible plot directions remain marked as unresolved.</p>`},
{id:'peoples',label:'Peoples & alliances',title:'Different cultures. The same inheritance.',lede:'Sun and Dark Elves are not opposing forces of good and evil. Their differences grew from competing ideas about how to live with their fey nature.',body:`<div class="grid"><div class="info"><span class="tag">SUN ELVES</span><h3>The discipline of light</h3><p>Beautiful, refined, and deeply invested in duty, reputation, and tradition. Their cities favor pale stone, open skies, gardens, symmetry, and monumental towers. They see themselves as the stable continuation of ancient elven civilization.</p></div><div class="info lunar"><span class="tag">DARK ELVES</span><h3>The freedom to change</h3><p>Equally beautiful, they value intuition, emotional honesty, adaptability, and individuality. They embrace the uncertain parts of their inheritance and remember their ancestors as survivors of persecution.</p></div><div class="info"><span class="tag">HUMANS</span><h3>Dynasties of borrowed magic</h3><p>Allied with Sun Elves through faith and interwoven family trees. Humans have no natural magic without fey ancestry, making elven marriages extraordinarily valuable to noble houses. Admiration can coexist with resentment of elven influence.</p></div><div class="info lunar"><span class="tag">DWARVES</span><h3>The living stone</h3><p>An ancient subterranean people devoted to Gaia. Stone, metal, roots, fungi, and underground rivers all belong to the living world. Craftsmanship and mining carry spiritual weight. Their friendship with Dark Elves is matched by a long memory of Sun Elf invasions.</p></div></div><h3>The absent ancestors</h3><p>The fey once lived close enough to humanity to have children together. Those descendants became elves. No people holds a verified explanation for the fey’s disappearance; myths and contradictory fragments are all that remain.</p>`},
{id:'history',label:'The ancient schism',title:'History has more than one witness.',lede:'The first elves belonged to one civilization. A disagreement about their future became a rupture that shaped the world.',body:`<div class="timeline"><section><h3>A shared beginning</h3><p>Human and fey ancestry produced the first elves, who inherited magic through their blood.</p></section><section><h3>A philosophical divide</h3><p>One tradition sought to discipline fey nature through order and restraint. Another believed suppressing that nature was dangerous, embracing instinct, dreams, and change.</p></section><section><h3>Belief becomes violence</h3><p>Religious and political identities hardened. The ancestors of the Dark Elves were driven from their lands, and many fled underground.</p></section><section><h3>Strangers in dwarven lands</h3><p>Dwarves initially saw the refugees as trespassers. Understanding their flight led to practical cooperation, then trust, then an enduring alliance.</p></section><section><h3>Invasions remembered</h3><p>Sun Elf forces pursued fleeing populations toward dwarven territory. Later rulers claimed underground resources by divine or ancestral right. Dwarves remember invasion; Sun Elf accounts often minimize or justify it.</p></section><section><h3>An uneasy peace</h3><p>Trade and diplomacy survive alongside historical grievances. Neither modern elven civilization possesses the complete truth of the schism.</p></section></div><div class="note"><p><strong>An unresolved chronology</strong><br>The fey vanished long ago. The exact timing and its relationship to the schism have not been established.</p></div>`},
{id:'divine',label:'Gods & inherited magic',title:'The gods are real. Magic is older.',lede:'The Sun and Moon are complementary forces. Centuries of mortal belief have made their differences seem irreconcilable.',body:`<div class="grid"><div class="info"><span class="tag">THE SUN GOD · NAME UNDECIDED</span><h3>Light & order</h3><p>Worshipped by Sun Elves and humans. Associated with light, fire, civilization, truth, protection, and hope.</p></div><div class="info lunar"><span class="tag">THE MOON GOD · NAME UNDECIDED</span><h3>Cycles & possibility</h3><p>Worshipped by Dark Elves. Associated with night, dreams, destiny, change, emotion, magic, and fertility.</p></div></div><h3>Gaia, the living world</h3><p>For dwarves, Gaia is the consciousness of the planet: nature, life, and stone. Gaia is likely older than the solar and lunar traditions, and regards both forces as necessary to the natural world. The exact divine relationships and degree of intervention remain open.</p><h3>One source, two traditions</h3><p>Elven magic comes from inherited fey blood, not an original gift from the gods. Training and culture shape how that power is expressed.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Tradition</th><th>Approach</th><th>Expressions</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sun Elf</td><td>Precision, structure, control</td><td>Light, wards, formal spellcraft</td></tr><tr><td>Dark Elf</td><td>Intuition, emotion, adaptation</td><td>Illusion, dreams, transformation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="note"><p><strong>The divide is cultural, not biological.</strong><br>A Sun Elf can learn Dark Elf magic, and vice versa—a fact that challenges both peoples’ claims to fundamental difference.</p></div>`},
{id:'bloodlines',label:'Bloodlines & belonging',title:'The same struggle. Two answers.',lede:'Full-blooded elves have great difficulty conceiving with one another. This shared reality has shaped marriage, family, and power in very different ways.',body:`<h3>The Sun Elf answer: dynastic planning</h3><p>Elven–human fertility is much closer to the human baseline. Sun Elves frequently enter arranged marriages with human nobility, creating heirs with magical potential and binding ruling families across generations.</p><p>For human houses, fey ancestry is political capital. Carefully tracked genealogies and selective marriages consolidate magic, prestige, and access to elven leadership. Love may develop, but it is not always the deciding consideration.</p><h3>The Dark Elf answer: shared continuity</h3><p>Dark Elves developed communal fertility traditions rather than relying on widespread intermarriage. The monthly Lunar Masquerade loosens rank and courtship conventions, while children are accepted regardless of whether both biological parents are known.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Sun Elf culture</th><th>Dark Elf culture</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Reproduction</td><td>Human intermarriage and dynastic planning</td><td>Communal traditions and lunar celebrations</td></tr><tr><td>Belonging</td><td>Genealogy, inheritance, family ties</td><td>Households, extended kin, community</td></tr><tr><td>Uncertainty</td><td>Something to organize and control</td><td>Something to accommodate and accept</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="note"><p><strong>Neither system is inherently superior.</strong><br>Both grew from the same difficulty. The romance asks which inherited values each protagonist will choose to keep.</p></div><span class="status">UNRESOLVED</span><p>The cause of low elven fertility, half-elven fertility, and the way magical inheritance changes across generations are still unknown. Greater fertility between fated mates remains a possible direction, not an established rule.</p>`},
{id:'underworld',label:'Life beneath the earth',title:'Beneath the surface, a living world.',lede:'The great underground city belongs to Dark Elves and dwarves together. Its name is still undecided. Its character is unmistakably shared.',body:`<h3>A city built in every direction</h3><p>Elven towers descend from cavern ceilings. Dwarven halls cut into immense stone walls. Suspended bridges cross underground rivers, while geothermal lakes, luminous fungal forests, roots, crystals, and foundries fill the depths.</p><p>Magical transportation and dwarven engineering connect neighborhoods whose traditions have blended for centuries. The city is not a pair of segregated districts. It is one interwoven civilization: intimidating from afar, warm and communal within.</p><div class="note"><p><strong>A nature faith beneath the earth</strong><br>For dwarves, a cavern is as much a part of nature as a forest. The city exists within Gaia’s living world.</p></div><h3>The Lunar Masquerade</h3><p>Once each lunar cycle, Dark Elves hold a masked celebration of fertility, renewal, and the continuation of their people. Masks obscure social rank; for one night, wealth, profession, and lineage are meant to matter less.</p><p>Participation is voluntary and adult. Anonymous or semi-anonymous intimacy and conception are accepted parts of the tradition. Within Dark Elf culture, the Masquerade is a respected institution, not a scandal.</p><h3>Children of the community</h3><p>Children conceived during the Masquerade are fully accepted, even when parentage is uncertain. Extended households and community support can take precedence over strict biological lineage. Specific inheritance rules remain to be developed.</p><p>Dwarven neighbors, mentors, teachers, and honorary relatives form part of Dark Elf children’s everyday lives. Dwarves may join the festivities socially, while the fertility traditions primarily concern Dark Elves.</p>`},
{id:'bond',label:'Fated mates',title:'Recognition is not surrender.',lede:'A rare remnant of fey magic connects two people across a divide their societies insist should remain intact.',body:`<div class="grid"><div class="info"><span class="tag">WHAT THE BOND DOES</span><h3>A profound recognition</h3><p>Emotional resonance. Heightened awareness of one another. Unusual magical compatibility. A powerful sense that the other person matters.</p></div><div class="info lunar"><span class="tag">WHAT THE BOND DOES NOT DO</span><h3>Erase the choice</h3><p>The bond predates modern religion and does not originate with either the Sun or Moon god. People can resist it, even if they cannot easily ignore it.</p></div></div><h3>One bond, four interpretations</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><tbody><tr><td>Sun Elves</td><td>A sacred connection that can contradict dynastic plans and religious prejudice.</td></tr><tr><td>Dark Elves</td><td>An ancient fey inheritance to respect, rather than an unquestionable command.</td></tr><tr><td>Dwarves</td><td>A practical challenge to the idea that politics can overrule an ancient connection.</td></tr><tr><td>Humans</td><td>Views differ; noble houses may resist bonds that disrupt magical marriages.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>The romance at the center</h3><p>One Sun Elf. One Dark Elf. Their connection puts love, ancestry, faith, and obligation into conflict. They bring different assumptions about intimacy, marriage, family, and permanence.</p><span class="status">POSSIBLE STORY DIRECTION</span><p>The Sun Elf may already be promised to a powerful human house. Choosing their mate could threaten succession, bloodline plans, diplomatic agreements, and elven political interests. The protagonists’ names, roles, first meeting, and recognition of the bond remain undecided.</p><div class="note"><p><strong>The question is bigger than whether they belong together.</strong><br>What do they owe the lives and communities they already belong to?</p></div>`},
{id:'mysteries',label:'The unanswered',title:'What the world has forgotten.',lede:'Some truths are disputed. Others have not yet been written. These questions remain open in the story bible.',body:`<details open><summary>Why did the fey vanish?</summary><p>No civilization knows. Myths, fragments, and contradictory folklore survive. Connections to magic, the gods, the schism, fertility, or fated bonds remain possibilities rather than answers.</p></details><details><summary>What truly happened during the schism?</summary><p>Both cultures preserve biased accounts. What evidence survives that might challenge them—and who, if anyone, knows the complete history?</p></details><details><summary>Why do fated bonds exist?</summary><p>Their fey origin is established; their original purpose is not. Exact recognition mechanics and the possibility of heightened fertility between mates remain to be developed.</p></details><details><summary>Why is elven fertility so low?</summary><p>Was it always this way? Did the fey have the same limitations? Is it connected to their disappearance? The fertility of half-elves is also unresolved.</p></details><details><summary>How does magical ancestry change?</summary><p>How much fey ancestry does human magic require? Does power weaken over generations? Can noble houses consolidate near-elven strength? Forgotten bloodlines and false genealogies are possible story directions.</p></details><details><summary>How well do mortals know their gods?</summary><p>The Sun and Moon are not true enemies, but their exact relationship and their capacity to intervene remain undecided. Their names have not yet been chosen.</p></details><details><summary>Who will carry this story?</summary><p>The protagonists’ identities, obligations, and first meeting remain open, as do the names of nations, human kingdoms, and the underground city. The Masquerade’s inheritance rules and the limits of elven lifespan and magic also need definition.</p></details>`}
];
const nav=document.querySelector('#chapter-nav'),content=document.querySelector('#chapter-content');let current=0;
nav.innerHTML=chapters.map((c,i)=>`<a href="#${c.id}" data-chapter="${c.id}"><span>${String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')}</span>${c.label}</a>`).join('');
const chapterArt={
overview:['eclipse-world','TWO CIVILIZATIONS · ONE ANCESTRY','An eclipse over contrasting elven civilizations'],
peoples:['sun-elf','THE PEOPLES · CULTURE & IDENTITY','A Sun Elf before golden towers','portrait'],
history:['eclipse-world','THE ANCIENT SCHISM · A DISPUTED HISTORY','Golden and violet elven cities divided by a deep chasm'],
divine:['fey-magic','THE INHERITED ARTS · OLDER THAN RELIGION','Gold and violet spellwork converging between two elven hands','magic'],
bloodlines:['sun-elf','BLOODLINES · FAMILY & POWER','An elaborately dressed Sun Elf','portrait'],
underworld:['underground-city','THE UNDERGROUND ALLIANCE · A SHARED CIVILIZATION','A luminous city combining dwarven halls and hanging elven towers'],
bond:['fey-magic','FATED MATES · RECOGNITION & CHOICE','Two elven hands reaching through interwoven magic','magic'],
mysteries:['eclipse-world','THE UNANSWERED · FRAGMENTS OF A LOST WORLD','An eclipse suspended over an ancient fantasy landscape']};
function render(id,focus=false){
 let i=chapters.findIndex(c=>c.id===id);if(i<0)i=0;current=i;const c=chapters[i],art=chapterArt[c.id];
 content.innerHTML=`<h2>${c.title}</h2><p class="lede">${c.lede}</p><figure class="chapter-art ${art[3]||''}"><img src="${art[0]}.webp" alt="${art[2]}" width="1536" height="1024"><figcaption>${art[1]}</figcaption></figure>${c.body}`;
 document.querySelector('#chapter-number').textContent=`CHAPTER ${String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')}`;
 document.querySelector('#chapter-count').textContent=`${String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')} / 08`;
 nav.querySelectorAll('a').forEach(a=>{if(a.dataset.chapter===c.id)a.setAttribute('aria-current','page');else a.removeAttribute('aria-current');});
 document.querySelector('#next-chapter').innerHTML=`${i===chapters.length-1?'Back to the world':'Next: '+chapters[i+1].label} <span>→</span>`;
 if(focus){content.focus({preventScroll:true});document.querySelector('#chapters').scrollIntoView({behavior:matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches?'instant':'smooth'});}
}
document.addEventListener('click',e=>{const a=e.target.closest('[data-chapter]');if(!a)return;e.preventDefault();const id=a.dataset.chapter;history.pushState(null,'','#'+id);render(id,true);});
document.querySelector('#next-chapter').addEventListener('click',()=>{const id=chapters[(current+1)%chapters.length].id;history.pushState(null,'','#'+id);render(id,true);});
window.addEventListener('popstate',()=>{const id=location.hash.slice(1);if(chapters.some(c=>c.id===id))render(id,true);});
render(location.hash.slice(1));if(chapters.some(c=>c.id===location.hash.slice(1)))requestAnimationFrame(()=>document.querySelector('#chapters').scrollIntoView());
const magicTraditions={
 sun:{label:'THE SOLAR TRADITION',title:'Give the unknown a shape.',text:'Precise wards, luminous spellcraft, and disciplined forms. Sun Elves refine their inherited power through structure and control.',traits:['Wards','Light','Precision']},
 moon:{label:'THE LUNAR TRADITION',title:'Let the unknown become.',text:'Illusion, dreams, and transformation. Dark Elves work with intuition and emotion, adapting to the unpredictable nature of their inheritance.',traits:['Illusion','Dreams','Transformation']},
 fey:{label:'THE COMMON ORIGIN',title:'The blood remembers.',text:'The fey vanished, but their magic endures in their descendants. Both elven traditions draw from this same inheritance. Neither holds exclusive claim to its possibilities.',traits:['Ancestry','Shared power','Fated bonds']}
};
const magicTabs=Array.from(document.querySelectorAll('[data-magic]'));
function showMagic(kind){const item=magicTraditions[kind],panel=document.querySelector('#magic-panel');magicTabs.forEach(button=>{const active=button.dataset.magic===kind;button.setAttribute('aria-selected',String(active));button.tabIndex=active?0:-1;});panel.setAttribute('aria-labelledby',`${kind}-tab`);panel.innerHTML=`<div class="eyebrow">${item.label}</div><h3>${item.title}</h3><p>${item.text}</p><div class="magic-traits">${item.traits.map(t=>`<span>${t}</span>`).join('')}</div>`;document.querySelector('#magic').dataset.tradition=kind;}
magicTabs.forEach((button,index)=>{button.addEventListener('click',()=>showMagic(button.dataset.magic));button.addEventListener('keydown',event=>{let next;if(event.key==='ArrowRight')next=(index+1)%magicTabs.length;else if(event.key==='ArrowLeft')next=(index+magicTabs.length-1)%magicTabs.length;else if(event.key==='Home')next=0;else if(event.key==='End')next=magicTabs.length-1;else return;event.preventDefault();magicTabs[next].focus();showMagic(magicTabs[next].dataset.magic);});});
let progressQueued=false;
function updateProgress(){const range=document.documentElement.scrollHeight-innerHeight;document.querySelector('#reading-progress').style.width=`${range>0?Math.min(100,Math.max(0,scrollY/range*100)):0}%`;progressQueued=false;}
window.addEventListener('scroll',()=>{if(!progressQueued){progressQueued=true;requestAnimationFrame(updateProgress);}},{passive:true});window.addEventListener('resize',updateProgress);updateProgress();
